Motivation
==========

- Make and use own language is fun and rewarding
- Main use: games. Need some of the flexibility (and ease of coding) of Lua (for rapid coding of game logic), and speed of compiled languages (for performance on data)
- Simple design, lightweight syntax: less to know, easier to use
- Built-in useful types like variable sized array, vector2d/3d, hash table, list, with appropriate syntactic sugar for quick use
- Automatic glue for calling C libraries: access to libc, SDL, OpenGL, etc.

Design
======

- Object oriented: everything is an object, all functions are methods (<- Ruby), except the basic types "number" and "pack"
- All operators (binary and unary) are in fact syntactic sugar for method calls, and are therefore user definable, except for numbers and packs
- Introspection: objects know their own class, which methods they answer to, etc, which allows for late binding when type is not known at compile time, and "true" virtual methods (-> SmallTalk, ObjC). The only time we know type is when the object has been created in the same function. Otherwise it could be a child of the "apparent" type. (But it can be checked at linking whether the given class has children or not for more optimisation)
- Classes know their name as a string
- No globals
- Variable declaration is automaticly done at first assignment, and type is infered from it
- Class definition and implementation are separate: "natural" private and public members.
- "import" command includes only class definitions from given file, while "include" is pure text insertion. Allows class def. and impl. to be in the same file without inclusion dependencies problems.
- Arrays/Lists are homogenous: can contain different types only if they are children of the base type
- for loops are either numeric, or iterative over arrays/lists/user defined classes
- Methods can return several values (<- Lua), but it is part of their signature. Methods with similar names can have a different number of return values (ex: iterators with just values or index(es) + value)
- Multivalued assignments (<- Lua) (?? Not so useful, except for swap and multi return values, that can be handled separately)
- Variables are always references to objects (except for numbers, which are not objects). Method parameters and return values are always by reference.
- Forbid aliasing! There can be only one ref to an object at a given time. Possession can either be passed to another variable (and own value is set to nil), or abandonned, and object is killed. ( ????? Solves memory management. Might be unpractical at usage. But cool. ) When passing to functions, the final value of the local ref is passed back automatically to the caller (incl. if it has been nil-ed by destruction or storage somewhere else).

This means: existence of an object is determined by non-nil-ness of *its* (a) reference to it. Magic. No more NULL pointer dereference, or problem of freeing a pointer within a function and still having an address out of it. Also, nice deleting syntax "a = nil".

This forced ownership gives memory a natural tree-like structure (which is what I do most of the time anyway). Deleting an object automatically deletes all the objects it references, and so on. Similarly, copying an object forces a deep copy (otherwise, the copied object would have all its members set to nil). There is even no need to overload the copy method, since it's always done the same way (?)

For the few cases where we actually need multiple references to objects (ie: graphs structures), have one vector own them all, and use the indices for referencing.

Plus, it allows über optimisations at compilation: within a LLMS function, there can be only one "variable" holding the object, even if the high level variable changes.

- Bitwise operations defined for numbers. Converted to and back from uint32. 64 not necessary since double cannot hold a full int64. A long double could, though (configurable?)
- Can use as scripts: automatically enclosed in main() if none found
- A "pack". Kind of struct without named fields, that can be used without declaring it. Still type checked when adding it to arrays or passing to functions. Always copied (like numbers), cannot be refered to. Allows tight packing in arrays without the class overhead (pointer to class). Accessed using numerical indexes (like would do for heterogenous lists in Lua). Anything "bigger" or which needs named fields or pass by reference should be a class (even a methodless class, which would still inherit init, and copy and such)
- "unreachable" return function.
- Automatic string<->number coercion when type cast is forced by function call (otherwise, available as method)
- "symbols": hashed string casted to num for quick comparison (<- Ruby, Lua). For instance FVN-32. Regular strings can be hashed if need be but there are character restrictions
- association tables?
- numbers can also be "nil" (floating point representation of NaN). Allows omiting parameters in method calls?
- 0 and num nil evaluate to false in a condition, true otherwise. obj nil to false, true otherwise. (overloadable to_bool() ?)
- Possibility to extend existing classes, notably built-in (compensate the absence of functions that would act on these)
- Have a root class to allow total polymorphism by defining methods there (?). Also has the interface for init, copy, == (automaticly deep and built-in!), class, isa, etc.
- self and super keywords (<- Objective-C) with the interesting possibility of doing self = nil and commiting suicide, while niling the outside reference (self is passed as double-ref argument too!). Or self = other. Maybe give them restricted use, like assignment only at end of method to avoid "object change" mid-method. Or make it possible and that's fun.
- No variable shadowing (<- C#). Fits well that there is no declaration syntax either. If it already exists, it's the outside one, else, it's a new local one. Lexical scoping, though (lose stuff at block end).
- Fancy var-in-string replacement with light syntax. No ${aha}.

- Collections provide the iter() method that returns an iterator object, that itself answers to next(). Return type of iter() tells the compiler which object it is, and return type(s) of next() for the local variables. next() itself can have several forms (like returns value or index + value).
	for a in v do ... end
	--->
	it = iter v
	a = next it
	while a do
		...
		a = next it
	end

Multi iterator solution: if the given object answers to next() consider it the iterator. Else, look for iter().

	for l in lines file do end
	for l in words file do end
	for c in chars file do end

- This reminds: the construct with two similar assignment with a while loop, due to the fact that an assignment has no value, is kind of ugly. All the "producer" type objects that give nil at the end should provide the iter() method to be iterated on with a general for loop.

Lexing
======

- Keywords are fixed, of course
- Class names start with a capital letter
- Variables are alpha num + underscore (start with small alpha or underscore)
- Method names are operators or like var names
- Rest should be numbers: decimal, hexa (0x), scientific
- Strings with var replacement are in "...", raw unescaped strings in '...'
- Internized symbols as @blah (or something)
